WEBVTT

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.845
A merchant prices work in U S D C on Solana, but the client holds U S D C on Base.

00:00:06.845 --> 00:00:09.684
SolSettle lets the merchant set the amount to receive.

00:00:09.684 --> 00:00:14.732
Here is a real, funded test of that invoice, including a small return to the
payer.

00:00:15.492 --> 00:00:18.659
The public application has its own Aurora integration key.

00:00:18.659 --> 00:00:24.643
In Live quotes, enter half a U S D C and request the current exact output price.

00:00:24.643 --> 00:00:29.570
The browser discovers native Base and Solana U S D C, then calls Aurora.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.738
This preview returns an estimate and no payment address.

00:00:33.498 --> 00:00:39.133
For the funded invoice, the merchant supplied a Solana receiving wallet, and the
payer supplied a Base refund wallet.

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:47.734
A fresh Aurora quote required zero point seven four eight eight eight eight U S D
C on Base for exactly half a U S D C on Solana.

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:51.589
The payer checked the instructions and signed the transfer in their own wallet.

00:00:52.349 --> 00:00:54.707
After the deposit, Aurora reported success.

00:00:54.707 --> 00:00:59.815
SolSettle bound that status to the signed quote originally issued for this
invoice.

00:00:59.815 --> 00:01:08.459
It then checked successful execution, the canonical U S D C mint, the receiving
owner, token transfers, and the net balance increase.

00:01:08.459 --> 00:01:11.603
Five hundred thousand atomic units reached the merchant.

00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:13.175
The invoice is paid.

00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:18.629
The destination link opens the same transaction in the official Solana Explorer.

00:01:18.629 --> 00:01:20.977
It shows success and finalized confirmation.

00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:24.107
The signature and slot identify this particular payment.

00:01:24.107 --> 00:01:29.976
The merchant can inspect the transaction without trusting a number typed into the
invoice interface.

00:01:30.736 --> 00:01:34.193
The same successful swap also included a partial refund.

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:37.266
Aurora reported three hundred fifteen atomic units returned.

00:01:37.266 --> 00:01:45.716
A separate successful Base transaction contains a native U S D C transfer for
precisely that amount to the original refund wallet.

00:01:45.716 --> 00:01:48.021
This was an actual on-chain return.

00:01:48.021 --> 00:01:49.941
The Solana invoice stayed paid.

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:54.425
Judges can reproduce the result from the funded receipt.

00:01:54.425 --> 00:01:56.907
Select Recheck Aurora and both chains.

00:01:56.907 --> 00:02:06.422
SolSettle reads the provider status again and compares the original quote terms,
the Base deposit, the finalized Solana credit, and the Base refund.

00:02:06.422 --> 00:02:09.317
Each row opens the corresponding public transaction.

00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:10.972
Rechecking moves no funds.

00:02:11.732 --> 00:02:13.896
An on-chain success alone is insufficient.

00:02:13.896 --> 00:02:21.831
Here the independent verifier checks the actual destination transaction against a
larger expected amount, zero point five one U S D C.

00:02:21.831 --> 00:02:27.602
The credit is still half a U S D C, so the check reports a shortfall.

00:02:27.602 --> 00:02:30.848
This separate inspection leaves the original paid invoice intact.

00:02:31.608 --> 00:02:35.344
The repository includes setup instructions and twenty-six automated checks.

00:02:35.344 --> 00:02:38.665
The separate underfunded, full-refund example is labelled simulated.

00:02:38.665 --> 00:02:44.478
The Base deposit, Solana credit, and partial Base refund in this walkthrough are
real.

00:02:44.478 --> 00:02:48.214
The project receipt lets you recheck each of them.

